            <ArticleTitle>Involvement of Neuroactive Steroids in Pain, Depression and Anxiety.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>94-102</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1159/000435935</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Comorbidity between major depressive disorder (MDD), anxiety (generalized anxiety, panic disorder, social anxiety disorder) and pain is a major complicating factor in the diagnosis and treatment of psychiatric and neurological disorders. Although numerous neurotransmitters and/or neuromodulators may be involved, abnormalities in the GABAergic and glutamatergic systems seem to be a common factor in all these disorders. Neuroactive steroids (NASs) have been the object of considerable interest in this area in recent years since they appear to act predominantly on GABA-A and glutamate NMDA receptors. An overview of the possible involvement of NASs in MDD, anxiety and pain is provided in this chapter.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2015 S. Karger AG, Basel.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
